{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 biographical drama film directed by david fincher, written by grant heslov, benjamin weissman, and catherine cornwell, and produced by scott rudin and morgan freeman. the film is based on the book 'the facebook effect' by douglas kramer.\n\nthe movie tells the story of mark zuckerberg, a brilliant but awkward college student who created the social media platform facebook. the film focuses on the early days of facebook, including zuckerberg's college years at harvard university, where he met his future business partner and wife, edgar mccartney, and his roommate, derrick zhang.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, armie hammer as derrick zhang, and ausie joey king as erin andrews. the supporting cast also includes todd jefferson, rachel mcadams, and max jungmann.\n\nthe social network explores themes of friendship, loyalty, and the consequences of success. the film portrays zuckerberg as a complex and multifaceted character, driven by a desire to create something new and innovative. however, the film also highlights the darker side of zuckerberg's personality, including his treatment of others and his willingness to do whatever it takes to achieve his goals.\n\none of the standout aspects of the social network is its cinematography. the film's use of bold colors and stark lighting creates a sense of tension and unease, perfectly capturing the mood of the characters. david fincher's direction is also noteworthy, as he balances humor and drama with ease, creating a film that is both entertaining and thought-provoking.\n\nthe social network received widespread critical acclaim, with many praising its performances, direction, and screenplay. the film was nominated for eight academy awards, including best picture, best director, and best actor for jesse eisenberg's portrayal of mark zuckerberg.\n\noverall, the s